WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.755 --> 00:00:06.130
We'll jump in,
and we'll continue

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.390
where we are last time.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.210
And weirdly, this picture
tells you everything.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.210
This gives you all the
insight you need to know.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:14.945
It's just weird and hilarious.

00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:15.445
Whoops.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.460
Let me fix this.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.180
Yeah.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.790
So here we're just
minimizing a quadratic.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.290
We know exactly
what the minimum is.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.970
It's just x equals 0.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.030
So we just try something
like the steepest-- here

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.240
we're using gradient descent.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.240
It's just simple enough.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.323
You can actually work out
what the iterations are.

00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:40.590
And what you'll find--

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.150
I mean, you have
a formula for it,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.962
but the picture
is basically this.

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.560
These curves show
you the level sets.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.055
And what you can see
pretty clear-- you're

00:00:52.055 --> 00:00:53.430
beginning to see
it already here,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.730
is if the level sets
are poorly conditioned,

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.820
meaning they're anisotropic.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.770
Isotropic means you're the
same in all directions.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.752
Anisotropic means
you're not the same.

00:01:04.752 --> 00:01:06.460
You're different in
different directions.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:07.835
So if your curvature
is different

00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:10.380
in different directions,
or another way to say it,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.730
if your sublevel sets
are not nearly spherical,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.827
then gradient descent
works quite poorly.

00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:18.660
And you can see why.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.640
It does this weird zigzagging.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.960
OK.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.730
And then the flip
side of this is

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.720
how well does gradient descent
do when the sublevel sets are

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.950
actually literally spherical
or balls, Euclidean balls?

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.600
Here the answer is
just-- it's one step.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.280
OK.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:44.710
So takeaway message is that
the efficiency or speed

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.930
of gradient descent
is going to depend

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:53.080
on how aspherical or anisotropic
the sublevel sets are.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.620
And here is just an
example to show it works.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.370
I mean, it's an example
in two dimensions.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:03.340
It's silly just to show that
you are getting zigzagging

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.770
or something like that.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.340
And I think we looked
at this last time.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.710
And now it comes to--

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.340
so the conclusion is,
depending on the problem,

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.870
if your sublevel sets are very
well conditioned, or spherical,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:24.070
or symmetric, or however you
want to say it, or isotropic,

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:29.260
or something like that, then
gradient descent is fine.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.265
So what this hints
though is that there's

00:02:32.265 --> 00:02:33.390
something strange about it.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.180
And it's actually a bit weird.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.890
Because when you
think about it, why

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.850
would you not use the
the negative gradient

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.543
as your descent direction?

00:02:44.543 --> 00:02:46.210
Just something seems
very weird about it

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.550
because you arrive at
a point, and then you'd

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:52.102
say, hey, if I was to move a
small amount in any direction,

00:02:52.102 --> 00:02:54.310
which would be the direction
where my objective would

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.182
go down the fastest?

00:02:56.182 --> 00:02:57.640
And it seems like
you'd be an idiot

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.900
to take anything other
than that direction.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:03.040
And so we had said, well,
it's the negative gradient.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.050
When you analyze that
statement further,

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:10.430
it turns out that depends
on the metric you're using.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.733
So it actually depends
on the norm you use.

00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:14.150
And so the way you
think of that--

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.440
one way to do this in a
principled way is to say, well,

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.330
the amount--

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.120
if you go in a
small direction v,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.510
then the amount f
is going to decrease

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:30.410
is about equal to the
gradient of f transpose v. You

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.640
want that negative.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.890
So then you'd think,
well, OK, take v equals

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.840
minus the gradient.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.340
That's an obvious choice.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.260
You can't just say
choose v to minimize

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.770
grad f of x transpose v. You
can't do that because it's

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:46.550
unbounded below.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.058
It's silly.

00:03:49.058 --> 00:03:50.600
And here's where
the metric comes in.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.580
You have to give a
norm to limit that.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.928
You have to say, no, I
want the norm of v to be,

00:03:56.928 --> 00:03:58.970
let's say, equal to 1 or
less than or equal to 1.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.440
It would be the same thing.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.610
So you do that.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.570
And here-- now, notice
that this norm up here on v

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:06.560
is a general norm.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.720
It is not the
two-norm necessarily,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.500
just a general norm.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.440
So this is referred to as the
steepest descent direction.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.589
And it's going to
depend on the norm.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:20.060
And then you can unnormalize it.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.100
It's actually convenient
to normalize it

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.970
by the dual norm
of the gradient.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.840
And then you get something
that looks like that.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.290
So the steepest descent
method is basically--

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:34.670
and it's specified by a norm.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:35.630
You give a norm.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.860
So once you give a norm, then
the steepest descent method

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.370
is completely specified because
that's the direction you're

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:44.510
going to use.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:48.770
And you get that the convergence
is similar to this descent.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:52.430
But we'll look at some
examples to understand this.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.920
So the first one
is if you just--

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.110
what is steepest descent
in the Euclidean norm?

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:01.650
It's precisely gradient descent.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:02.150
Yeah?

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.600
Why do you normalize it by
the dual norm of the gradient?

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.820
You actually don't have to.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.430
There's no reason
to normalize it.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.170
It's just so that a
lot of the equations

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.670
get simpler if you do
the convergence analysis.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:15.170
Yeah.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:16.550
So that's the only reason.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:20.780
Also that way, like for example,
the steepest descent direction

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.440
with the L2 norm is literally
the negative gradient.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:26.880
Otherwise, it would be the
negative gradient scaled.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.490
It'd be a unit vector.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.320
It actually would be
the negative gradient

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.640
divided by the norm of the
gradient, so unit vector.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.400
So if you use a quadratic norm--

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.920
so a quadratic norm is
you take a quadratic form

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.565
with a positive definite matrix.

00:05:42.565 --> 00:05:43.440
Take the square root.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.050
That's a quadratic norm.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:48.275
And here's an example of a--

00:05:48.275 --> 00:05:51.900
I guess this is a unit ball
in a quadratic norm, which

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.190
is an ellipsoid.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.870
And then it says here's
the negative gradient.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.113
And it says you should
minimize the inner product of--

00:05:59.113 --> 00:06:01.530
or sorry, you should-- yeah,
you should minimize the inner

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.750
product of a point in
this set with the--

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.470
I guess minimize it
with the gradient,

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.410
which means maximize
the inner product

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:11.620
with the negative gradient.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:18.360
So basically, it says these
are the hyperplanes that

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.440
are going to be--

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.010
this is the outward normal.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.180
These are the level curves.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.430
And it says you want to go
as far as you can this way.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.260
And you end up here.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:32.083
And so this is, in fact, the--

00:06:32.083 --> 00:06:33.750
this is going to be
the steepest descent

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:37.110
direction in the norm
where this is the unit

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.730
ball we're showing you, OK?

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:42.610
Now, you can work things out
like you're never going to--

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.960
so actually, it does
change the direction.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:45.850
It's very weird.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.000
It changes the
direction, never rotates

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.070
it more than 90 degrees.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.670
It cannot, obviously, because
then you wouldn't have a--

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:58.540
you'd have a positive inner
product with the gradient.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.590
So this is it.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.290
So weirdly-- so
what the real answer

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.080
is it says that when you stop
and you ask for directions,

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:08.530
and you say what direction would
be the direction where you go

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.280
downhill the fastest, the
person should ask you, yeah,

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.420
in what norm?

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:16.420
Because what you select as
a norm is going to change

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.430
the directions they give you.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:24.650
And you can see that it
distorts the directions.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:25.880
OK.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:28.160
It's actually super interesting.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.820
If you do the L1 norm,
what you'll work out

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:35.300
is actually also a
very simple thing.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.720
If you take the inner
product of a gradient

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.110
with a vector whose L1
norm is less than 1,

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:43.550
you want to minimize that.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:47.690
What you will do is you will
find the entry, the axis

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.000
along with where the
partial derivative

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.750
or entry of the gradient
is most negative.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:54.950
And then you'll go--

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:55.790
sorry.

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:58.520
You find the largest one
and then go in, obviously,

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.635
the decreasing direction.

00:08:00.635 --> 00:08:02.510
And so that's actually
got a-- that's a named

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:04.940
method from the '40s and '50s.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.650
And it basically--

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:09.230
I mean, that one has got
a very simple story to it.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:12.440
You stop where you
are, and you say--

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:16.280
then you ask not which direction
is the steepest direction

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:16.820
to go down--

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:17.750
I mean, you can.

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:18.570
It's in the L1.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:23.130
But what you really ask is
you ask, if I go along axis 1,

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:24.870
how much am I going to go down?

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.120
If I go along axis 2, how
much is it going to go down?

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:28.902
And you find the
one where you're

00:08:28.902 --> 00:08:30.360
going to go down
the most, and then

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.665
you simply go along the axis.

00:08:31.665 --> 00:08:34.020
So that's a so-called
axis-aligned step,

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.159
people would call it.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:36.600
OK.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.840
And again, the picture
shows how you actually

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:41.520
get a different direction.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.250
OK.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:48.930
Now, the choice of the metric
or the norm for steepest descent

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.790
is going to make
a big difference.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.150
And here's just this
baby example, again,

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:57.030
where this is-- the first one
shows you steepest descent

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.820
with a norm whose unit
ball looks like this.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.337
And I don't know.

00:09:01.337 --> 00:09:02.170
You just eyeball it.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.680
You can see some
number of iterations.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.950
And here we take
steepest descent

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:13.380
in using a norm where the
unit ball is skewed this way.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.170
And what you see is
that has exacerbated

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.630
the convergence horribly.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.750
And so what this
tells us is the--

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.780
it tells you the obvious thing.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.110
It says that what
you really want

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.320
is you want a norm, or
a metric, or something

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.370
like that that's aligned
with the sublevel sets

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:39.030
of your function.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:42.760
In other words, it's one which
has roughly the same shape.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:45.130
So this is just a very
simple example to show this.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:49.570
I'm just pretending that
this function looks fatter

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:51.940
than it does look tall.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:57.730
And so over here this use of
norm, this selection of norm

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.590
actually improves
the convergence.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:02.830
This one exacerbates it, OK?

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:06.100
Does this make sense?

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.200
These are just ideas just to--

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:13.000
I mean, but it's the essence
of where we're going.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.390
Because now we go to the--

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.190
if you keep going
with this logic

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.440
you end up at something
called the Newton step.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.320
So the Newton step,
you actually will use--

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.520
what you'll do is
you take the Hessian

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:35.260
of the second derivative of the
function at the current point.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:40.360
And you then use
that as your metric.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:42.280
And so the argument now
makes perfect sense.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.660
Because if I said--

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:47.170
if you say, OK, I accept the
idea that I should choose,

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:50.470
let's say, a metric
that captures

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:53.565
the shape of my function,
that's what I should do.

00:10:53.565 --> 00:10:54.940
Actually, let's
have a discussion

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:56.690
about smooth functions
near their minimum.

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:58.490
Let's have that
discussion just for fun.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:01.000
What do smooth functions look
like, smooth convex functions

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.208
look like near their minimum?

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:07.710
What do they look like?

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:09.150
They're just quadratic.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.740
And they're quadratic with--

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:13.830
what's the Hessian
there of the--

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.840
I mean, well that
that gives it away.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.430
That's the Hessian.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:20.400
The sublevel sets, if
this is a smooth convex

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:21.930
function, that's the minimizer.

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.930
And then I say what do
the level sets look like?

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:25.770
They look like this.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.380
Now, these are not exactly
ellipsoids, I mean,

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:30.450
unless the function
is exactly quadratic.

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:32.850
But this is what they look like.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.340
I mean, eventually they start
looking not like ellipsoids.

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.250
They start looking like that.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:39.700
Because who knows?

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:43.020
But down near the minimum,
they look really very much

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.600
like ellipsoids.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.820
And the shape of
the ellipsoid is

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.140
determined by the Hessian of
the function at this point.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.130
OK.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.230
By the way, if
you're in statistics

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.960
or if you're not in statistics
but know some statistics,

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.250
you'd recognize
this immediately.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.460
If you look at the
negative log likelihood,

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:04.160
that's going to be--

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.718
and this is in your
parameter space.

00:12:05.718 --> 00:12:07.760
This point is going to be
your maximum likelihood

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:12.110
or minimum negative
log likelihood point.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:16.160
And then if I ask you, well,
tell me about models that have

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.910
a likelihood almost as high
as my maximum likelihood--

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:21.770
everybody following this--

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.150
then basically I'm looking at--

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:25.100
I'm looking exactly
at level curves

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:27.560
that are very close to
the maximum likelihood.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:31.340
I pulled down from the maximum
likelihood, and I get sets.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.800
Now, these are
parameters and models

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.830
that fit the data you've seen.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:38.190
What do they look like?

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.870
Well, if the negative
log likelihood

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:47.540
is convex and smooth,
then they look-- they're

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:50.180
approximately ellipsoidal.

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:53.210
And so actually--
and the ellipsoid

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:55.370
is determined by the Hessian
of the log likelihood

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.340
at the maximum likelihood point,
which is, of course, the Fisher

00:12:58.340 --> 00:12:59.330
information.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:00.390
Everybody got that?

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.780
And so this tells
you very good ways

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.990
to get sets of model
parameters which

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.360
give you almost the maximum
likelihood on the data

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.310
you've seen.

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:12.550
Again, if you know
about this, fine.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:14.730
And if you don't, that's OK too.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:17.610
I'm just saying that
this idea is everywhere.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:19.080
OK.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.000
So now we connect it back to
this conversation and said,

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.010
here's what you should do.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:28.770
You should use steepest
descent, but what you should do

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:31.710
is you need to get
a metric that looks

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:35.370
the curvature of your function.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:39.570
So this would be a
stunningly good method.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:40.920
Ready for my method?

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.090
It says do steepest descent.

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:47.170
And someone says, yeah,
which quadratic norm

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:48.420
are you going to use, which p?

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:51.150
And I would say, oh, the
Hessian of your function

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:52.800
at the optimum.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.360
Everybody follow this?

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:55.710
Boy, would that work well.

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:57.690
Everybody got that?

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:00.930
That would work
unbelievably well.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:04.290
Only minor problem is
someone says, OK, well what

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:05.700
is the Hessian at the optimum?

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:08.040
And then the answer would be
something like, well, first

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.180
minimize it, and
then I'll tell you

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.430
the Hessian, which is kind
of like this is stupid

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:12.930
because that's what I
want to do in the first--

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:14.010
everybody follow this?

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:16.980
OK, so instead
we're simply going

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.980
to use the Hessian
at the current point

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:23.310
as the best approximation
we can make of the Hessian

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:24.880
at the optimal point.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:26.950
Everybody following this?

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:29.290
That's one way to explain
what Newton method is.

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:37.442
And if we take the Newton
step and each time we redo it,

00:14:37.442 --> 00:14:38.900
we're actually
changing the metric.

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:41.230
And so sometimes people
refer to this-- this

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:44.440
is a variable metric method.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.260
Because you're actually--
you're changing the metric

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:50.300
when you stop and ask for
directions and say, hey,

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.630
what's the fastest way
to go down from here?

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:58.060
And then someone on their toes
says, yeah, in what metric?

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:00.750
Every step you
take, you change it.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:03.360
OK.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.070
I mean, these are
just ideas or ways

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:07.030
you can think about
what the Newton step is.

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:10.290
There are lots of other
ways to think of it too.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:15.300
So here's another
completely beautiful method.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:19.770
It says let's have a
second-order approximation

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.180
of our function.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:23.700
And that's, of course,
just nothing but that.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:27.810
And you'd say, all right,
let's minimize that.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.060
If you form a second-order
approximation of a function

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:33.450
at a point, minimize it,
you get the Newton step.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:34.860
Everybody got that?

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:38.670
So that also just
makes perfect sense.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:40.530
Yeah.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:42.090
Another way is you
could think about

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.320
the optimality conditions,
and that looks like this.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:45.090
So this would be here.

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:46.570
Here's a picture
of the first one.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:49.650
So here's the function f.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.240
We're at x here.

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:55.830
Now, what I do is I get the
second-order approximation

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:56.550
here.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.950
This is f hat of f.

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.180
By the way, the
first-order approximation

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:03.190
has to be an
underestimator of f.

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:04.960
The second order, we don't know.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:06.640
Could be above or below.

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:08.920
You don't know.

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.910
OK, so here you are here.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:14.710
Here you are here.

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:15.790
We get a second order.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.190
And what it means to be a
second-order approximation

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:20.680
is that f and f
hat right near here

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:26.500
are extremely close, which is
my way of saying close cubed.

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:31.600
It's small cubed,
something like that.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:33.530
They agree to second order here.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:36.520
So it's a super good
approximation right here.

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:37.908
OK, so we form that.

00:16:37.908 --> 00:16:38.950
That's this dashed thing.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.650
And you can see over here
it's on the wrong side.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:42.910
It's a lower bound up here.

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:43.780
It's an upper bound.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:45.310
And the minimizer
of this is this.

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:47.710
And so this is the Newton
step, the difference

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:49.120
between here and here.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:50.470
OK.

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:53.530
Another one is you linearize
the optimality condition.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:56.560
And you'd say, I'm at a point x.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.750
I want a point where
the gradient of f is 0.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.123
And you go, cool, then
what are you going to do?

00:17:02.123 --> 00:17:04.040
And you say, well, I'm
going to perturb x by--

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:05.810
I'm going to take a step v.

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:08.210
But what you'd like is
you'd like the gradient

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:12.339
of f of x plus v to equal 0.

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:14.780
But you don't know how
to solve that in general.

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:16.030
There's one case where you do.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:17.984
We'll get to that in a minute.

00:17:17.984 --> 00:17:19.359
Instead you'd say,
well, you know

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:21.151
what, I'll approximate
this using calculus.

00:17:21.151 --> 00:17:23.470
So what this gradient is--

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:26.829
this is the first-order
expansion of the gradient.

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:30.280
The gradient here--
this is going

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:31.760
to be the first-order thing.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:32.990
It's actually the following.

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:35.770
It's the gradient plus the
second derivative times

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.510
the displacement.

00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:39.640
And then what you
do is you say, OK, I

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:43.100
don't know what's actually
going to happen to the gradient.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.530
But this I know.

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:46.370
This I get.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:47.840
I can compute these two.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:49.700
And I'll just solve
this equation.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:51.950
And that gives us,
again, the Newton step.

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.620
So that all make sense?

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:56.660
I mean, I hate to give--

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:58.790
there are even
more explanations,

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:02.570
but these are just
interpretations of what it is.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:04.820
OK, so that's the Newton step.

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:08.600
Oh, and here's a picture
of the second one in 1D.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.150
So actually, Newton would
have been quite familiar

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:12.920
with this picture.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.130
So here's the derivative.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:19.460
It's increasing because
the function is convex.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:20.043
So here it is.

00:18:20.043 --> 00:18:22.502
And what we want to do is we
want to find the zero-crossing

00:18:22.502 --> 00:18:23.100
like this.

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.050
So we want to find
that point there.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:26.550
So how do you do that?

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:29.270
Well, you're at a point
here, and you simply

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:32.270
here take a linear,
I guess you would

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:36.830
say, or affine approximation of
the derivative at that point.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:38.300
That's this dashed line here.

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:42.980
And then wherever that is
0, that's our next point.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:45.750
By the way, if I were to do
it again, what would happen?

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:51.290
So if I went to this point,
calculated the first-order

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:54.590
approximation, and looked at the
0 of it, what would your next--

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.050
if I iterated the
Newton step twice,

00:18:57.050 --> 00:18:59.495
I think your eyeball is
going to tell you what?

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:05.260
Are you going to get
a good approximation?

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:06.580
Yeah, good.

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:09.040
And then if you do
it a third time,

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:12.130
it's going to be
really, really good.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:12.820
Why?

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.640
Because if I zoom in on a
smooth increasing function right

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:19.420
as it goes through 0, if I Zoom
in it just looks like a line.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:21.190
That's all it looks like.

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:23.080
The same is true over here.

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.690
If I take this point,
fit a quadratic to it,

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:29.800
and minimize it-- but suppose
when I get near the bottom here

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:34.570
and I fit a quadratic to it,
how good an approximation

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:37.910
is the quadratic nearby?

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.310
It's really good.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:46.400
And so all of this is giving you
this intuition that this method

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:51.620
should have some amazingly good
terminal convergence, which

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:54.820
we'll get to in a minute, OK?

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:56.710
And it's all true.

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:57.880
OK.

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:01.970
And here's another
very cool way to do it.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:06.610
It turns out it's the steepest
descent direction in the metric

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:09.670
given by the Hessian.

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:15.220
And that makes total sense
because, again, you land at x.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:18.950
You ask someone, hey, what's the
steepest way down the mountain

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:19.450
or whatever?

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:23.830
And then they ask you, in
what norm or what metric?

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:27.730
And you say, well,
do you happen to know

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:30.733
the Hessian of the function
at the minimizer down

00:20:30.733 --> 00:20:31.900
at the bottom of the valley?

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:33.880
And they'll say, no, I don't.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:37.550
I just live here or
something like that.

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:38.920
Then you'd say, fine.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:40.420
Do the Hessian at
the current point?

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:42.590
And they would say,
oh, that I do know.

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:43.090
OK.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:45.550
You go, fine, we'll use that.

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:46.900
Everybody following this?

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:48.850
And that would give you this.

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:50.950
So it's very cool,
and you can actually

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:54.170
plot pictures that shows
what a Newton direction is

00:20:54.170 --> 00:20:56.320
going to look like.

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:58.070
And this is an example
showing that you're

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:01.160
going to get a pretty
good Newton step, at least

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:02.660
for this thing.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:04.370
OK.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:05.750
So this is the Newton step.

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:11.270
Then you have something cool
called the Newton decrement.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.400
The Newton decrement, you
can think of it this way.

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:19.310
A very good way to
think about it is this.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:23.120
Actually, it is the predicted
decrease when you take a Newton

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:26.620
step of the function,
a court predicted

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:29.240
by your quadratic
model of the function.

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:30.190
So that's what it is.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:31.510
Yeah?

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:34.690
Can you define again what the
metric defined by the Hessian

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:35.450
means?

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:35.950
Oh, yeah.

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:39.220
It means I'm using a
quadratic metric, which

00:21:39.220 --> 00:21:40.540
is going to be this.

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:44.492
When someone says,
what's your norm?

00:21:44.492 --> 00:21:45.700
And I'd say, oh, that's easy.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:47.810
I'll take the norm to be this.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:49.150
This is my current point.

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:50.980
So here, this is the norm of z.

00:21:55.370 --> 00:21:56.330
There you go.

00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:57.260
That's it.

00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:58.080
Yeah.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:58.955
That's what it means.

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.620
By the way, if you
have other ways

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:08.490
to get a rough idea of the
shape of your sublevel sets,

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:09.660
by all means use it.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:12.645
I mean, there's lots of other
ways you might be able--

00:22:12.645 --> 00:22:15.180
there are plenty of cases where
you might have some other--

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.190
be able to get
another estimate which

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.920
is not the Hessian or something
like that to get this.

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.140
So OK.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:23.550
All right.

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:28.020
So one thing that's very
cool about this metric

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:31.080
is that it turns out
it's lots of things,

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.640
but one thing that's
very important

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:35.320
is it's actually
affine invariant.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:37.600
So that's actually going to
be a big, important theme.

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:44.670
So if I change coordinates,
if I apply an affine transform

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:47.460
to my problem and solve that--

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:50.730
by the way, if you do
that with gradient method,

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:53.760
that's actually completely
equivalent to using

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.772
steepest descent with a
different quadratic norm.

00:22:56.772 --> 00:22:58.480
So you can even think
of steepest descent

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:02.900
as two steps, where the
quadratic norm is two steps.

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:12.910
Step one, apply a matrix so
that your unit ball becomes just

00:23:12.910 --> 00:23:14.710
a Euclidean ball.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.360
Another way to say it is--

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:18.490
actually, some people
use a great verb

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:23.980
for that, which is you round the
problem, which is a great term.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:27.790
Because to round it means
if your sublevel sets look

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:30.310
like this and you apply
a coordinate transform,

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:33.310
and then you just want
it to look isotropic.

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:35.890
You want the curvature
to be about the same

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:37.270
in all directions.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:38.470
And someone says, why?

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:41.020
You'd say because
when the curvature is

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:45.250
the same in all directions,
gradient method is outstanding.

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:47.050
Everybody got this?

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:47.905
So OK.

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:54.850
So here, if you
change coordinates

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:57.800
by a linear mapping,
this doesn't change.

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:00.830
It's the same number.

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:05.080
So affine invariance is
going to come up later.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:05.740
OK.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:07.160
So now we have Newton's method.

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:10.940
So I mean, it just
uses the Newton step.

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:12.940
So you compute the Newton
step and the gradient.

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:17.770
You quit if this
so-called Newton

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:19.940
decrement is small enough.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.200
Then you use a backtracking line
search, for example, whatever.

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:30.550
And this entire algorithm
is basically independent.

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:33.400
It commutes with
changes of coordinates.

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:41.680
So in other words, if someone
says, instead of minimizing f,

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:43.660
I'm going to minimize
this f tilde, which

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:47.410
multiplies its argument by
a non-singular matrix T.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:50.110
In gradient method, you get
completely different iterates.

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:52.388
And all that might be
faster, might be way slower.

00:24:52.388 --> 00:24:52.930
I don't know.

00:24:52.930 --> 00:24:53.920
It just depends.

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:56.080
But you'll get a
completely different thing.

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.570
Actually, up here
it all goes away.

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:00.910
And you can check
because when you do this,

00:25:00.910 --> 00:25:03.760
the gradient gets
a T in front of it,

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:07.790
and then the Hessian gets a T
on the left and a T transpose

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:09.510
on the right or
something like that.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:11.120
And when you
assemble everything,

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:12.390
it all just goes away.

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:15.230
So you get the
identical steps here.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:17.540
So another way to say
this, Newton's method

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:20.090
is independent of
changes of coordinate,

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:23.540
of any linear change
of coordinates.

00:25:27.005 --> 00:25:31.070
That's an extremely
good attribute to have.

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:33.470
Actually, in theory it's
cool, but it's actually

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:36.320
super useful in practice.

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:39.020
What it means is to
first order, scaling

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:41.800
doesn't matter for something
like a Newton method.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:44.790
It doesn't make any
difference at all.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:47.700
I mean, if you were going to do
the linear algebra and infinite

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:51.390
precision, it literally wouldn't
matter at all, whereas that's

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:55.140
completely false for something
like a gradient method.

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.060
There you change coordinates.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:01.350
And if you change
coordinates, you either

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:04.290
get something faster or,
actually much more likely,

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:06.000
slower.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:06.510
OK.

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:09.900
So this is Newton's method.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:12.630
So I'm going to give you the
classical convergence analysis.

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:14.660
So this is actually interesting.

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:18.020
This is actually from the
Soviet Union around the 1930s.

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:20.780
But here it is.

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:22.610
It's actually super interesting.

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:26.180
Oh, and actually
before we start,

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:29.527
I have to ask you a
bunch of questions.

00:26:29.527 --> 00:26:31.985
How well does Newton's method
work on a quadratic function?

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:38.410
Yeah, quite well.

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:41.110
Yeah, how well?

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:43.090
It converges in one step.

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:43.750
Why?

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:46.660
Because one way to understand
what Newton's method is you

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:49.930
say you're at x, and you
say, please develop for me

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:52.180
a quadratic approximation
of your function.

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.610
What's a quadratic approximation
of a quadratic function?

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:55.640
Itself.

00:26:55.640 --> 00:26:57.873
And then the next step
is it says minimize that.

00:26:57.873 --> 00:27:00.040
So actually, while you're
computing the Newton step,

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:01.665
you didn't know it,
but you're actually

00:27:01.665 --> 00:27:02.890
solving the entire problem.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:04.330
Oh, and by the way--

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:07.040
yeah.

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:07.540
Yeah.

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:09.880
Notice the other
thing is that when

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:12.920
I ask you to minimize a
quadratic convex function,

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:14.910
what does that turn into?

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:16.650
It's linear algebra.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:23.010
It's nothing else because the
Hessian is affine in the point.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.610
So it's just linear algebra.

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:27.360
And so therefore, that means
I can pull in all the stuff

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:28.800
we talked about last week.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:29.687
And we will.

00:27:29.687 --> 00:27:30.520
We'll do that later.

00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:31.437
That's another thread.

00:27:31.437 --> 00:27:34.320
But we'll be talking about
what does it mean for a Hessian

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:39.150
to be diagonal, or
banded, or sparse?

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:41.580
And we'll talk about
all of that later.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:43.380
So what it says is--
so I mean, one way

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:48.690
to say it is computing a
Newton step is basically

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:50.900
linear algebra.

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:53.250
You know a fair amount
of linear algebra by now.

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:55.980
You know at least
the beginnings of it.

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:57.600
And it says it's
just linear algebra.

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:00.210
So we've reduced solving
a problem when you're

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:02.850
doing Newton's method to
a sequence of problems

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:05.550
in each step you
use linear algebra.

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:06.720
It's cool.

00:28:06.720 --> 00:28:08.118
OK.

00:28:08.118 --> 00:28:08.910
Let's get back to--

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:10.660
OK, so we established
that Newton's method

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:14.940
works shockingly well
for quadratic functions.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:20.910
So what's really going to
affect Newton's method actually

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:23.610
is how well-- remember
how this all went.

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:24.780
It all went this way.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:27.270
You wanted to use
the metric which was

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:31.810
the Hessian at the solution.

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:35.450
Instead you use the
Hessian where you are.

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:36.880
And by the way,
that also explains

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:38.710
why this is going
to do really well as

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:41.290
you get closer and closer
because that approximation is

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:43.150
going to get better and better.

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:44.660
OK.

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:49.125
So when you're here, when
you're right close to,

00:28:49.125 --> 00:28:51.250
let's say, if this is a
maximum likelihood problem,

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.560
if you're right close
to the parameters,

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:55.870
then the Newton method
is going to give you

00:28:55.870 --> 00:29:00.460
essentially an extremely good
estimate of the parameter

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:02.230
on the next step.

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:04.420
Everybody got this?

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:06.710
OK.

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:07.210
All right.

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:14.050
So what it depends on is
actually the third derivative,

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:16.660
if you think about it carefully.

00:29:16.660 --> 00:29:19.600
It's the third
derivative that matters.

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:21.490
Because the third
derivative tells you

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:25.460
how much the second derivative
changes when you move.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:27.700
That's what the
third derivative is.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:30.890
So if the third derivative
of a function is small,

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:32.800
then our intuition is
that Newton's method

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:34.540
should work unbelievably well.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:35.740
And someone would say, why?

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.943
And you'd say,
well, because it's

00:29:37.943 --> 00:29:39.860
getting close to what
you really wanted to do,

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:43.540
which was to change
coordinates or how

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:45.100
you use a metric
which was aligned

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:47.710
with the Hessian defined by
the Hessian at the optimum.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:49.585
And you'd say, but you're
not at the optimum.

00:29:49.585 --> 00:29:51.370
Yeah, but the Hessian
changes very slowly.

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:53.530
So I mean, there's
not more to what I'm

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:56.230
saying than this is not deep.

00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:58.690
Everybody got this?

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:01.090
Here's an extreme case of a
third derivative being small.

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:02.700
It's 0.

00:30:02.700 --> 00:30:05.430
What does it mean if a
third derivative is 0?

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.600
It's quadratic.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:10.800
So all of us should be expecting
it's a third derivative.

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:12.780
Now, the third
derivative of a function

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:15.570
on r is something none of
us should be afraid of.

00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:21.283
Third derivative of a
function on rn is something--

00:30:21.283 --> 00:30:22.700
this has to do
with weirdness, has

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:26.660
to do with our education in
mathma-- we should all avoid.

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:29.360
It's a trilinear form.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:32.498
So if you're cool with tensors--

00:30:32.498 --> 00:30:34.790
people in mechanical engineering
can do this very well.

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:35.990
A lot of people in
physics can do it

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:38.407
because they're used to working
with higher-order tensors.

00:30:38.407 --> 00:30:39.740
They don't have any friends.

00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:41.990
And they're like,
they don't scare me.

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:42.740
What do you mean?

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:44.510
And I'm like, there's
three indices.

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:47.342
And they're like, yeah, and so?

00:30:47.342 --> 00:30:48.800
Anyway, I'm just
saying, unless you

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:52.460
have it come from
some weird fields

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:55.560
where you do handle
these kinds of things,

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:57.560
then it's complicated.

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:01.970
For the rest of us who have this
weird education where we were

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:04.610
trained about matrices--
that would include me--

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:08.750
then we get very uncomfortable
when we go above two indices.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.220
Everybody following this?

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:13.536
So if you're in that-- are
you in one of these fields?

00:31:13.536 --> 00:31:14.410
Yeah.

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:15.410
Which one?

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:15.910
Physics.

00:31:15.910 --> 00:31:16.660
There you go, physics.

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:16.870
See?

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:18.450
Yeah, they laugh at
us, and they say, well,

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:19.325
that's contravariant.

00:31:19.325 --> 00:31:20.590
That's covariant.

00:31:20.590 --> 00:31:22.790
I don't care how many of
these Einstein-- it's fine.

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:24.100
It's totally fine.

00:31:24.100 --> 00:31:24.940
It's fine.

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:26.980
So wouldn't bother you to take--

00:31:26.980 --> 00:31:28.360
yeah, how about this?

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:31.870
How about the third derivative
of the log determinant

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:35.040
of a positive definite matrix?

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:37.890
You can pretend to visualize it.

00:31:37.890 --> 00:31:38.390
Yeah.

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:40.110
OK, good.

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:40.610
OK.

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:41.480
So yeah.

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:43.940
I mean, that's some
crazy trilinear form

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:46.760
that's got six indices
because the original thing is

00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:47.420
indexed by two.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:48.950
Anyway, fine.

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:50.510
All right.

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:53.547
All right, so actually it turns
out all we need to express

00:31:53.547 --> 00:31:55.130
is that the third
derivative is small.

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:58.790
So we're not going to actually
work with third derivatives

00:31:58.790 --> 00:31:59.933
and trilinear forms.

00:31:59.933 --> 00:32:02.600
And we're certainly not going to
talk about the third derivative

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:05.480
of, let's say, the
negative log determinant

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:08.480
of a positive definite
matrix, which, I mean,

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:09.690
I could write it out.

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:10.880
I can write out all the
partial derivatives.

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:11.850
I know what they are.

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:14.845
I just don't want to write it
down because of my training.

00:32:14.845 --> 00:32:15.470
So that's fine.

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:16.620
OK.

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:17.120
OK.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:18.890
So instead this
is often expressed

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:20.450
as a Lipschitz constant.

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:22.970
So this is cool.

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:24.080
This we can do.

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:29.780
If I take the two-norm of the
difference of the two Hessians

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:32.240
and I say that
that's less than L,

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:33.980
so it has a Lipschitz
constant of L,

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:37.730
then that's basically
the same as saying

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:41.640
that the norm of
this trilinear form,

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:45.290
which is the third
derivative, is less than L.

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:48.912
And this avoids us having to
get a headache because there's

00:32:48.912 --> 00:32:50.620
three indices and all
that kind of stuff.

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:51.940
Everybody following this?

00:32:51.940 --> 00:32:53.820
And besides, this
is what you want.

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:58.140
So you should imagine that
if that condition holds and L

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:02.040
is small, Newton's method
should work really well.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:04.530
Because it says--
actually, that literally

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:07.530
says the Hessian doesn't
change much as you move.

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:11.970
And that's the key to Newton's
method working really well.

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:13.920
Everybody following this?

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:16.530
OK.

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:18.930
So here you have some
Lipschitz constant there.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.880
We'll assume that.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:23.790
And then the way--

00:33:23.790 --> 00:33:27.235
I won't go through the--

00:33:27.235 --> 00:33:28.860
I mean, if you read
the book-- it's not

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:30.818
even that long, but it's
not the kind of thing,

00:33:30.818 --> 00:33:32.550
I think, that should
be done in public,

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:34.710
is to walk through a proof
of this kind of stuff

00:33:34.710 --> 00:33:36.907
because I don't get it.

00:33:36.907 --> 00:33:39.240
The main point is-- the outline
is actually pretty cool.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:40.650
It basically says this.

00:33:40.650 --> 00:33:43.525
If the norm of the
gradient-- there's a number.

00:33:43.525 --> 00:33:45.150
And it says if the
norm of the gradient

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:47.610
is bigger than that
number, then it guarantees,

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:52.370
it absolutely guarantees a
fixed decrease in the function.

00:33:52.370 --> 00:33:54.170
That's cool.

00:33:54.170 --> 00:33:57.530
Once the gradient gets
less than that number,

00:33:57.530 --> 00:34:00.140
then you get something
crazy, which is that--

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:04.480
I mean, ignore the
constants, but basically

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:07.640
the norm of the
gradient, which is

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:12.080
how close are you to optimal,
that is less than or equal to--

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:16.159
it actually goes like
the square at most.

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:20.060
Now, to visualize what
happens, once the error

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:24.110
is 0.1, on the next
step it's 0.01.

00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:27.060
On the next step, it's 0.001.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:28.159
Wait, did I do that right?

00:34:28.159 --> 00:34:30.800
No, 0.0001.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:32.960
Next time, it's 1e minus 8.

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:35.239
And on the next step,
it's one 1e minus 16.

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:37.880
And now we're at floating
point precision, and we stop.

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:40.429
Everybody follow this?

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:42.710
And if you remember
linear convergence

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:44.777
from the gradient
method, linear--

00:34:44.777 --> 00:34:45.860
I mean, it's a weird name.

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:50.210
Linear was on a log
accuracy vertical axis

00:34:50.210 --> 00:34:51.380
and number of iterations.

00:34:51.380 --> 00:34:56.370
What that says is you get a
constant improvement each step.

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:59.310
And the improvement is
usually rather pathetic.

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:03.660
You multiply your error by 0.99.

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:05.940
You have to multiply things
by 0.99 for a long time

00:35:05.940 --> 00:35:07.290
until you get 1e minus 6.

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:09.366
Everybody following this?

00:35:09.366 --> 00:35:11.850
This accelerates.

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:16.860
The accuracy doubles every step
once you get into this region.

00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:19.770
And this is going to be
called quadratic convergence

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:24.900
because on a semi-log plot
it's going to be a quadratic.

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:26.070
OK.

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:29.430
And it's going to be the
way to understand that this

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:30.547
accelerates and gets--

00:35:30.547 --> 00:35:32.880
it's the thing we observed
just by looking at silly baby

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:36.030
examples that once you're
close to the optimum,

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:39.210
Newton's method is going
to work unbelievably well.

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:41.915
So OK.

00:35:41.915 --> 00:35:42.415
All right.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:48.730
So the first part is called
the damped Newton phase,

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:52.060
and the second is called the
quadratically convergent phase.

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:56.740
And in these cases,
most of the iterations

00:35:56.740 --> 00:35:59.710
require backtracking or damping.

00:35:59.710 --> 00:36:00.400
Yeah?

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:04.560
So I have a probably
naive question.

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:08.460
Can we understand that if the
property of the function f

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:12.040
is closer to the
quadratic function,

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:18.300
then the better the
convergence result?

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:20.160
That is a way to say that.

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:24.270
We expect here
that if L is small,

00:36:24.270 --> 00:36:26.730
then that's one way
of saying the Hessian

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:28.720
doesn't change as you
move around that much.

00:36:28.720 --> 00:36:29.820
That's what it says.

00:36:29.820 --> 00:36:30.600
Yeah.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:33.510
Then I would say that function
is closer to quadratic

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:35.050
than if L was really big.

00:36:35.050 --> 00:36:37.710
And so yes, we should
expect everything to work.

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:41.910
When L gets small, things
should work pretty well.

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:43.403
So yeah.

00:36:43.403 --> 00:36:44.820
I mean, it's
actually pretty cool.

00:36:44.820 --> 00:36:50.010
If L gets small, this
eta gets pretty big.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:51.810
And that means that
you get into the region

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:55.820
of quadratic convergence faster.

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:58.438
So indeed, it does go that way.

00:36:58.438 --> 00:37:00.230
But yeah, that's a
perfectly good-- how far

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:03.480
is the function from quadratic?

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:04.638
Because someone says, why?

00:37:04.638 --> 00:37:06.180
You say, well, for
quadratic function

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:07.900
it's game over in one step.

00:37:07.900 --> 00:37:12.490
And then this is a measure of
how far you are from quadratic.

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:14.640
OK.

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:15.900
All right.

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:23.520
So this first bit, it says since
you're guaranteed in this case

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:27.000
that you're going to go
down by this gamma thing,

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:29.340
then f of x0 minus p
star divided by eta

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:31.860
is the maximum
number of steps you

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:34.230
could be in the
damped Newton step.

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:37.240
Once you're here, you get
something that looks like this.

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:40.200
And I'm just going to point
out this is 1/2 to the 2

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.000
to the L minus k.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:45.210
So what happens is it
just keeps going down.

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:46.320
It accelerates.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:48.570
It's this thing where you
get four digits of accuracy,

00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:50.940
then eight, then 16.

00:37:50.940 --> 00:37:53.470
As a practical matter, you
don't do more than three

00:37:53.470 --> 00:37:55.950
or four steps in
quadratically convergent phase

00:37:55.950 --> 00:37:58.200
because then you just hit
machine precision and stop.

00:38:00.707 --> 00:38:03.040
And I'll tell you a story
about that a little bit later.

00:38:03.040 --> 00:38:09.145
OK, so this is the idea
behind the analysis here.

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:16.110
And the conclusion is
the number of iterations

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:19.860
until you get something that's
like epsilon suboptimal is

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:21.810
no more than the
current function

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:23.432
value minus the
optimal value divided

00:38:23.432 --> 00:38:25.140
by this number gamma,
which we could just

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:27.930
calculate in some number.

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:30.810
And then the quadratic
thing, since you're

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:34.350
doubling the number of basically
digits of accuracy each step,

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:36.420
it's going to be
proportional to log.

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:40.110
It's actually going to be
log log 1 over epsilon.

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:42.870
And you should read that
in a reasonable way.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:44.880
There's a very, very
good way to think--

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:48.030
bisection is like
log 1 over epsilon

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:50.610
because you divide your--

00:38:50.610 --> 00:38:53.400
and then even something
that multiplies your error

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:57.112
by 0.99, that's also like log 1
over epsilon because it just--

00:38:57.112 --> 00:38:59.070
I mean, it's got a
different constant in front,

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:01.110
but that's how long it takes.

00:39:01.110 --> 00:39:04.650
You get a constant fraction
improvement each step.

00:39:04.650 --> 00:39:06.990
The log log is because
when you get close,

00:39:06.990 --> 00:39:09.300
you start accelerating, and
you get faster and faster.

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:10.980
OK.

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.830
OK, so this is the-- and
that second term there,

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:16.950
I think a normal person, you
just read that second term

00:39:16.950 --> 00:39:19.890
as 3 or 5.

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:22.590
5 is generous.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:25.890
It's silly because
it's like once you're

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:28.830
in the region of quadratic
convergence, it's three steps

00:39:28.830 --> 00:39:31.870
and you're at double
precision stationary.

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:32.370
OK.

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:34.080
So OK.

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:37.390
Now, there's a few
problems with this.

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.140
And this is the
classical analysis.

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:40.680
OK.

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:43.020
There's a few problems
with this analysis.

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:46.200
Let's start with one.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:49.200
Let's see, gamma and epsilon 0.

00:39:49.200 --> 00:39:51.840
And they depend on m, L,
and the initial point.

00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:55.200
Now, do you imagine in
any practical problem

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:57.420
you would ever know
any of these things?

00:39:57.420 --> 00:39:59.040
The answer is no.

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:00.450
Are there cases where you do?

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:01.140
Yes.

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:04.230
If I'm doing logistic
regression or something

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:06.930
like that with some
L2 regularization,

00:40:06.930 --> 00:40:09.870
I do have some values
on some of these things.

00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:12.700
So that's the first thing.

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:13.750
OK.

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:18.430
And the other minor
point is p star

00:40:18.430 --> 00:40:20.818
appears in my
complexity analysis.

00:40:20.818 --> 00:40:22.360
So you're sitting
there, and you say,

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.490
I'm going to minimize this
function by Newton's method.

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.380
You say, but I'd like to
know how many steps it's

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:26.770
going to take.

00:40:26.770 --> 00:40:29.187
Or actually, what I really
need to know is an upper bound.

00:40:29.187 --> 00:40:30.820
And the person
says, yeah, awesome.

00:40:30.820 --> 00:40:31.540
It's that, OK?

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:33.550
It's f of x0 minus
p star divided

00:40:33.550 --> 00:40:35.230
by gamma plus log log epsilon.

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:39.500
And then you'd say,
OK, what's gamma?

00:40:39.500 --> 00:40:40.000
No one.

00:40:40.000 --> 00:40:41.480
You're not going to know gamma.

00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:42.730
You're not going to know that.

00:40:42.730 --> 00:40:44.320
And then you say, what's p star?

00:40:44.320 --> 00:40:47.050
And you go, oh, that's the
optimal value of the problem.

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:49.540
You're like, wait a minute.

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:50.350
So anyway.

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:54.520
So then what this suggests
is to actually use this--

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:55.947
I mean, I'm making fun of it--

00:40:55.947 --> 00:40:58.030
the answer is you should
first use Newton's method

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:02.080
to minimize the function,
whereupon you know p star.

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:05.560
Now you can actually go back
and evaluate this and go back

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:07.780
in a time machine and tell
yourself an upper bound

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:09.200
on the number of--

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:10.630
it's completely ridiculous.

00:41:10.630 --> 00:41:12.400
So when you make fun of it--

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:15.280
by the way, people taught
this like a crowning--

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:18.098
after two lectures of horrible
calculations you get to this,

00:41:18.098 --> 00:41:20.140
and it's supposed to be
the crowning achievement.

00:41:20.140 --> 00:41:21.220
Everybody following this?

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:27.410
So honestly to me, many
classical Western optimization

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:29.640
convergence results
sound like this.

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:31.760
Here's what they all
sound like to me.

00:41:31.760 --> 00:41:35.660
It says, if-- and there's a
long hypothesis filled with all

00:41:35.660 --> 00:41:38.570
sorts of stuff and parameters
that you will never know under

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:39.470
any circum--

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:41.030
there's no way.

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:42.680
If this is Lipschitz,
and there's L,

00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:44.210
and there's m, and strong--

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:45.460
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah--

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:50.660
then it will give you a bound
on the number of steps for that

00:41:50.660 --> 00:41:54.710
algorithm, which, by the way,
if you actually evaluated it

00:41:54.710 --> 00:41:57.580
with real numbers would be
numbers like 10 million or 10

00:41:57.580 --> 00:41:58.080
billion.

00:41:58.080 --> 00:41:59.600
It's completely useless.

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:01.160
And you go, yeah, thanks.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:03.860
Actually, my observation
is that this converges

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:05.510
in around 20 steps always.

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:08.890
So thanks for the upper
bound of 150 million.

00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:10.100
Thank you.

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:14.000
Anyway, it's just so it's-- they
all sound like to me if a bunch

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:17.030
of complete, utterly
unknowable hypotheses hold,

00:42:17.030 --> 00:42:21.950
then a completely useless
conclusion will hold.

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:26.870
That's how I hear basically
Western convergence analysis.

00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:29.515
And then when I confront
people and say this,

00:42:29.515 --> 00:42:30.890
they get very
angry, as you might

00:42:30.890 --> 00:42:33.800
imagine if that's all they
did their whole life was

00:42:33.800 --> 00:42:34.760
develop these.

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:36.590
And then they go,
look, you don't get it.

00:42:36.590 --> 00:42:38.420
I didn't want the number.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:41.030
You say, this is conceptual.

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:42.830
God, this guy is so dumb.

00:42:42.830 --> 00:42:44.450
The whole point of this--

00:42:44.450 --> 00:42:45.770
this is conceptual.

00:42:45.770 --> 00:42:48.290
This says if you
knew these numbers,

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:51.590
then I can prove it'll
take less than 150 million.

00:42:51.590 --> 00:42:53.330
Anyway, I don't buy it.

00:42:53.330 --> 00:42:54.400
I mean, it's fine.

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:56.400
It's better than knowing
it's not going to work.

00:42:56.400 --> 00:43:00.140
It's better than some fields
where you say, I don't know.

00:43:00.140 --> 00:43:01.310
Try it.

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:04.340
Although might have a little bit
more respect for some of those

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:05.610
fields anyway than the others.

00:43:05.610 --> 00:43:06.110
Anyway.

00:43:06.110 --> 00:43:06.610
OK.

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:08.570
Everybody following this?

00:43:08.570 --> 00:43:09.080
OK.

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:09.980
Yes?

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:12.860
If you can compute the
Hessian of your objective,

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:14.360
can you still adapt this method?

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:17.060
Perhaps you could iteratively--

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:17.750
Absolutely.

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:18.650
OK, so yeah.

00:43:18.650 --> 00:43:22.640
So if you can't compute
the Hessian at the point,

00:43:22.640 --> 00:43:24.500
what can--

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:25.520
OK.

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:29.120
Yeah, there's a lot of other--
there's whole fields you can

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:31.200
take, entire courses on that.

00:43:31.200 --> 00:43:35.390
So there are quasi-Newton
methods where you approximate.

00:43:35.390 --> 00:43:38.150
You build up an approximation
of the Hessian using only,

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:39.750
let's say, gradient information.

00:43:39.750 --> 00:43:41.010
That's one.

00:43:41.010 --> 00:43:43.358
There's a ton of
methods like that.

00:43:43.358 --> 00:43:44.150
It's a whole field.

00:43:44.150 --> 00:43:46.500
Actually, some of those
are really useful.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:48.870
So yeah, it's a good question.

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:51.950
There's also a lot
of other variations.

00:43:51.950 --> 00:43:54.020
Here's one that
actually is useful.

00:43:54.020 --> 00:43:57.800
You evaluate the Hessian
only every 10 steps.

00:43:57.800 --> 00:43:58.790
There you go.

00:43:58.790 --> 00:44:00.890
10 was the parameter,
but whatever.

00:44:00.890 --> 00:44:06.027
Then that actually makes perfect
sense, works unbelievably well.

00:44:06.027 --> 00:44:07.610
Now that you know
some linear algebra,

00:44:07.610 --> 00:44:08.630
you know the following.

00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:11.060
You compute the
Hessian, factor it,

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:13.380
and then when you're
computing Newton's steps

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:15.920
it's just a back solve,
and the cost is really low.

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:16.795
Everybody following?

00:44:16.795 --> 00:44:18.170
So there are a
lot of variations,

00:44:18.170 --> 00:44:19.587
but, yes, you're
absolutely right.

00:44:19.587 --> 00:44:20.540
There's that, yeah.

00:44:20.540 --> 00:44:27.710
And actually, some methods now
using Autograd and autodiff

00:44:27.710 --> 00:44:31.600
things, you just call dot
backward, dot backward.

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:32.100
OK.

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:34.680
And there are
methods that do that.

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:35.910
So that's even more radical.

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:38.490
That's like computer Hessian.

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:42.135
Why would you do that?

00:44:42.135 --> 00:44:45.277
I know how to do it with
autodifferentiation.

00:44:45.277 --> 00:44:47.610
So there's a lot of methods
that would be based on that.

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:48.990
You had a question?

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:52.230
I'm just curious-- where do
the optimization methods come

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:56.500
from that we do respect or use?

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:57.210
Oh.

00:44:57.210 --> 00:45:00.400
If all the ones are jokes,
then what are we using?

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:00.900
Oh, no.

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:01.400
Sorry.

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:02.730
The method is not a joke.

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:03.270
Oh, boy.

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:03.840
No, no, no.

00:45:03.840 --> 00:45:06.008
The analysis is a joke.

00:45:06.008 --> 00:45:07.050
To me, it's funny anyway.

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:08.525
Let's put it that way.

00:45:08.525 --> 00:45:09.900
I'm not making
fun of the method.

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:10.470
Oh, no.

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:10.970
Oh, boy.

00:45:10.970 --> 00:45:13.900
This works really
well in practice.

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:15.660
So the convergence analysis.

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:18.120
That's a joke to me personally.

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:19.260
That's a subjective thing.

00:45:19.260 --> 00:45:22.200
I mean, not to people
who this is all they do.

00:45:22.200 --> 00:45:26.640
But to me it's a little
bit of a joke, yeah.

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:27.750
But the method is not.

00:45:27.750 --> 00:45:28.890
Oh, no, no.

00:45:28.890 --> 00:45:29.940
Never confuse the method.

00:45:29.940 --> 00:45:31.720
Yeah, these are
different things.

00:45:31.720 --> 00:45:32.270
OK.

00:45:32.270 --> 00:45:32.770
OK.

00:45:32.770 --> 00:45:35.240
Now, there's something
even worse here,

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:36.520
which is an aesthetic.

00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:39.400
So I've made fun of this enough,
but I'm going to actually--

00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:41.960
I'm going to make
it even worse now.

00:45:41.960 --> 00:45:43.420
So here it is.

00:45:43.420 --> 00:45:48.140
The actual Newton's method
is affine invariant,

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:49.720
which is an amazing thing.

00:45:49.720 --> 00:45:55.090
The entire algorithm, if you
scale change coordinates and it

00:45:55.090 --> 00:45:57.850
takes 27 iterations,
you change coordinates,

00:45:57.850 --> 00:46:02.290
it takes 27 iterations, which
is an incredibly beautiful

00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:02.830
property.

00:46:02.830 --> 00:46:09.430
It says basically that it is not
sensitive in any way whatsoever

00:46:09.430 --> 00:46:11.868
to bad problem scaling and
all sorts of other stuff.

00:46:11.868 --> 00:46:12.910
Everybody following this?

00:46:12.910 --> 00:46:16.840
Beautiful, super useful in
practice attribute to have.

00:46:16.840 --> 00:46:17.950
OK.

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:20.530
OK, now you say, OK, this
is a beautiful algorithm.

00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:22.780
Could you please
analyze it for me?

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:24.280
And they come back.

00:46:24.280 --> 00:46:27.190
And they say, yeah, no problem.

00:46:27.190 --> 00:46:32.093
You need to tell me L,
and you need to tell me m.

00:46:32.093 --> 00:46:32.760
Yeah, that's it.

00:46:32.760 --> 00:46:33.990
OK.

00:46:33.990 --> 00:46:35.160
Everybody following this?

00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:38.790
And you'd say, excuse me,
if I change coordinates,

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:41.790
do the values of L and m change?

00:46:41.790 --> 00:46:44.000
And the answer is yes.

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:48.440
And now you have, aesthetically,
a deeply embarrassing

00:46:48.440 --> 00:46:49.490
situation.

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:51.680
Because somebody
who just wrote up

00:46:51.680 --> 00:46:53.690
the code in Rust
or C or something

00:46:53.690 --> 00:46:58.850
has this incredibly beautiful
thing, and the actual code

00:46:58.850 --> 00:47:04.160
itself is now more elegant
than the mathematical proof.

00:47:04.160 --> 00:47:06.980
Because the actual
code, it actually

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:10.310
is invariant up to
numerical issues.

00:47:10.310 --> 00:47:12.410
It's actually invariant
under a scaling or change

00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:13.190
of coordinates.

00:47:13.190 --> 00:47:14.330
But the proof is not.

00:47:14.330 --> 00:47:15.770
Everybody following this?

00:47:15.770 --> 00:47:17.630
So basically, what
would happen is I'd say,

00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:19.400
how long will it take
me to minimize this?

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:21.530
And they'll say, oh, I
don't know, couple million

00:47:21.530 --> 00:47:22.780
iterations, no more than that.

00:47:22.780 --> 00:47:24.140
Of course, then it takes 12.

00:47:24.140 --> 00:47:26.060
So then you're like, OK.

00:47:26.060 --> 00:47:28.100
And then you'd say the
exact same function.

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:30.330
And then you'd say,
if you don't mind,

00:47:30.330 --> 00:47:32.150
I'm going to change coordinates.

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:35.090
Well, the algorithm once again
solves it in 12 iterations

00:47:35.090 --> 00:47:37.173
because it's actually
affine invariant.

00:47:37.173 --> 00:47:38.840
And you go, what's
your upper bound now?

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:39.830
And they go, oh, now?

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:40.890
Oh, boy.

00:47:40.890 --> 00:47:43.650
15 million iterations for sure.

00:47:43.650 --> 00:47:46.597
I can prove it will take less
than 15 million iterations.

00:47:46.597 --> 00:47:48.180
And you go, well,
thank you very much.

00:47:48.180 --> 00:47:50.610
Just PS, it just took 12 again.

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:51.720
Everybody following?

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:53.130
So this is bad.

00:47:53.130 --> 00:47:54.630
This is not how the
world should be.

00:47:54.630 --> 00:47:56.850
The world should be like this.

00:47:56.850 --> 00:47:58.800
The math is beautiful and clean.

00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:00.490
That's how it should work.

00:48:00.490 --> 00:48:02.847
And then you go and you dig
deep into the source, which

00:48:02.847 --> 00:48:03.930
you probably shouldn't do.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:04.920
But if you do
this, you should be

