00:59:33.370 --> 00:59:34.930
itself.

00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:37.570
Because the actual algorithm
itself is affine independent,

00:59:37.570 --> 00:59:40.240
but then the analysis,
you change coordinates

00:59:40.240 --> 00:59:44.020
and they give you a different
number for their upper bound.

00:59:44.020 --> 00:59:46.630
I mean, still useless, but they
give you a different number.

00:59:46.630 --> 00:59:47.130
OK.

00:59:47.130 --> 00:59:50.575
So it turns out there's a
weird variation on this.

00:59:50.575 --> 00:59:55.390
This comes from the late '80s
and '90s from also Moscow.

00:59:55.390 --> 00:59:58.570
And it's developed by
Nesterov and Nemirovski.

00:59:58.570 --> 01:00:03.580
They have this 500-page--
no, 600, 700-page book.

01:00:03.580 --> 01:00:06.820
It's not for the faint of heart.

01:00:06.820 --> 01:00:12.370
They do look at third
derivatives of things

01:00:12.370 --> 01:00:14.560
like-- they look at the
third derivative of things

01:00:14.560 --> 01:00:17.750
like log determinant
of a matrix,

01:00:17.750 --> 01:00:20.600
positive definite matrix.

01:00:20.600 --> 01:00:25.370
That's the classical
Soviet math training.

01:00:25.370 --> 01:00:27.110
One of them--

01:00:27.110 --> 01:00:29.690
Nemirovski, actually
once told me-- he said,

01:00:29.690 --> 01:00:31.580
yes, we don't learn matrices.

01:00:31.580 --> 01:00:35.060
He said, for you it is a crutch.

01:00:35.060 --> 01:00:38.610
He said, it leads to
sloppy and weak thinking.

01:00:38.610 --> 01:00:41.300
And he said, we don't do
that until much older.

01:00:41.300 --> 01:00:44.460
He says, we just do
everything with indices.

01:00:44.460 --> 01:00:46.760
I was like, whoa, that's cool.

01:00:46.760 --> 01:00:48.070
So anyway, it's very--

01:00:48.070 --> 01:00:49.730
anyway, pretty crazy.

01:00:49.730 --> 01:00:50.520
OK, fine.

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:51.590
So here it is.

01:00:51.590 --> 01:00:54.530
And actually, it's
a hilarious thing.

01:00:54.530 --> 01:00:58.925
It actually replaces-- actually,
it is only the following.

01:00:58.925 --> 01:01:00.800
And then by the time I
tell the story enough,

01:01:00.800 --> 01:01:03.140
the solution is
going to be obvious,

01:01:03.140 --> 01:01:06.740
although this is just stunning
in the early '90s or whatever.

01:01:06.740 --> 01:01:10.970
Basically, the issue with
the classical analysis

01:01:10.970 --> 01:01:14.480
is that we had to say the
third derivative is small,

01:01:14.480 --> 01:01:19.040
but we said it in a way that
was not affine independent.

01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:21.680
That was the issue.

01:01:21.680 --> 01:01:25.890
Let's say it's a function
on just one variable

01:01:25.890 --> 01:01:28.410
so we don't have to worry
about tensors and three forms.

01:01:28.410 --> 01:01:28.910
OK.

01:01:31.785 --> 01:01:33.410
How do you say the
derivative is small?

01:01:33.410 --> 01:01:35.750
And here's the obvious one.

01:01:35.750 --> 01:01:39.300
You would say, how
about like this?

01:01:43.240 --> 01:01:43.740
There.

01:01:43.740 --> 01:01:45.492
That looks to me
like a statement

01:01:45.492 --> 01:01:46.950
that the third
derivative is small.

01:01:46.950 --> 01:01:49.890
And you go, cool.

01:01:49.890 --> 01:01:52.140
Now you say, let's do an
affine change of coordinates.

01:01:52.140 --> 01:01:55.230
And actually, this scales.

01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:57.550
So this is not a way--

01:01:57.550 --> 01:01:59.190
this is not an acceptable way.

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:01.565
I guess if I was going to use
the actual notation we were

01:02:01.565 --> 01:02:03.690
using before, it would be that.

01:02:03.690 --> 01:02:12.900
So this is not a way to say
the third derivative is small

01:02:12.900 --> 01:02:15.420
that is invariant under
changes of coordinates, OK?

01:02:15.420 --> 01:02:17.520
So now you have to
figure out how would you

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:20.850
say the third derivative
is small in a way

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:22.470
that when I change--

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:25.920
if I do a linear change of
coordinates it's invariant?

01:02:25.920 --> 01:02:29.160
So that's actually what
we're going to get.

01:02:29.160 --> 01:02:30.930
Actually, what happens
when you do this

01:02:30.930 --> 01:02:32.520
is the most stunning thing.

01:02:32.520 --> 01:02:36.060
Instead of a silly theorem
that looks like this-- if,

01:02:36.060 --> 01:02:38.940
and then a whole bunch
of absolutely unknowable

01:02:38.940 --> 01:02:41.010
hypotheses were to occur.

01:02:41.010 --> 01:02:44.160
And then you'd say then you'd
have a completely useless

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:44.850
conclusion.

01:02:44.850 --> 01:02:48.900
That's my template for a
traditional convergence result.

01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:51.480
Then it's actually
going to be very weird.

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:54.450
It's actually going to be if
a small number of actually

01:02:54.450 --> 01:02:57.880
weirdly knowable
things hold, then--

01:02:57.880 --> 01:03:01.570
and then the conclusions
are actually going to be--

01:03:01.570 --> 01:03:04.720
they're not going to
be hilariously wrong.

01:03:04.720 --> 01:03:07.030
They're not going to
be hilariously useless.

01:03:07.030 --> 01:03:07.780
So OK.

01:03:07.780 --> 01:03:08.800
So that's the idea.

01:03:08.800 --> 01:03:12.170
So we'll look at this.

01:03:12.170 --> 01:03:12.670
OK.

01:03:12.670 --> 01:03:16.340
And actually, we need to
use it later in the class.

01:03:16.340 --> 01:03:17.630
So OK.

01:03:17.630 --> 01:03:20.570
And they call this
self-concordance.

01:03:20.570 --> 01:03:22.390
So here it is.

01:03:22.390 --> 01:03:23.290
It's this.

01:03:23.290 --> 01:03:28.670
It says a function is
self-concordant if the absolute

01:03:28.670 --> 01:03:31.790
value is just from R to R. If
the absolute value of the third

01:03:31.790 --> 01:03:36.190
derivative is less than the--

01:03:36.190 --> 01:03:37.210
2 doesn't matter.

01:03:37.210 --> 01:03:38.920
That's just for convenience.

01:03:38.920 --> 01:03:42.190
The second derivative
to the 3/2 power.

01:03:42.190 --> 01:03:42.730
OK.

01:03:42.730 --> 01:03:45.760
So that's it.

01:03:45.760 --> 01:03:48.910
And the reason is this, is
this is affine invariant.

01:03:52.990 --> 01:03:57.580
If I replace this, if I
have f tilde of y is f of ay

01:03:57.580 --> 01:04:01.570
plus b, then what happens is
the third derivative scales

01:04:01.570 --> 01:04:05.710
like the cube of a and the
second like the square.

01:04:05.710 --> 01:04:09.010
If you take those numbers and
you plug it into that formula,

01:04:09.010 --> 01:04:11.050
the a's drop away.

01:04:11.050 --> 01:04:13.060
Everybody got this?

01:04:13.060 --> 01:04:15.950
So as a matter of
fact, as an exercise,

01:04:15.950 --> 01:04:19.330
if I just said right
away write down

01:04:19.330 --> 01:04:21.950
something that says the
third derivative is small,

01:04:21.950 --> 01:04:26.500
but it has to be invariant to
any affine change of variable,

01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:29.200
you would have to
come up with that.

01:04:29.200 --> 01:04:32.050
Everybody see that?

01:04:32.050 --> 01:04:33.850
You just have to.

01:04:33.850 --> 01:04:37.350
So I don't know if
this makes sense.

01:04:37.350 --> 01:04:39.600
Now, on the other hand, if
someone just walks up to me

01:04:39.600 --> 01:04:40.930
on the street and
says, yeah, I was

01:04:40.930 --> 01:04:42.222
thinking about Newton's method.

01:04:42.222 --> 01:04:43.780
I have a new
definition of small--

01:04:43.780 --> 01:04:47.110
sorry, I have a new definition
of the third derivative

01:04:47.110 --> 01:04:48.610
of small, or I have
a new definition

01:04:48.610 --> 01:04:51.070
of what it means to
be nearly quadratic.

01:04:51.070 --> 01:04:51.790
It's that.

01:04:51.790 --> 01:04:55.820
You would go like, where
did that come from?

01:04:55.820 --> 01:04:57.220
I mean, it's a bit shocking.

01:04:57.220 --> 01:04:58.180
So OK.

01:04:58.180 --> 01:05:00.145
Everybody got the idea?

01:05:02.810 --> 01:05:05.270
So some weird things happen.

01:05:05.270 --> 01:05:06.700
It turns out that a lot--

01:05:06.700 --> 01:05:13.930
certainly not all
convex functions

01:05:13.930 --> 01:05:16.090
are so-called self-concordant,
but a whole lot

01:05:16.090 --> 01:05:19.930
of the ones that
we encounter are.

01:05:19.930 --> 01:05:22.400
And anyway, so that's that.

01:05:22.400 --> 01:05:24.050
And you'd say, OK,
well, that's fine.

01:05:24.050 --> 01:05:27.890
You would expect a Newton's
method based on this to be--

01:05:27.890 --> 01:05:31.460
an analysis of Newton's method
based on self-concordance be--

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:32.870
you'd imagine it to be simpler.

01:05:32.870 --> 01:05:35.810
And you would be right.

01:05:35.810 --> 01:05:39.290
And there's a lot of
weird calculus stuff

01:05:39.290 --> 01:05:41.690
here you can actually
work about various things.

01:05:41.690 --> 01:05:43.460
But actually, it's
cool because you end up

01:05:43.460 --> 01:05:45.668
showing that a lot of the
things that we care about--

01:05:45.668 --> 01:05:47.660
here's one right here.

01:05:47.660 --> 01:05:51.410
So weirdly, it's
self-concordant,

01:05:51.410 --> 01:05:53.352
which would not be fun to show.

01:05:53.352 --> 01:05:54.560
It's not that hard, actually.

01:05:54.560 --> 01:05:57.320
To tell you the truth,
it's not that bad.

01:05:57.320 --> 01:06:00.120
And a bunch of other functions
are and things like that.

01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:02.750
So again, I'm not going to--

01:06:02.750 --> 01:06:05.180
this is not--

01:06:05.180 --> 01:06:09.770
I mean, it actually elucidates
why a lot of practical things

01:06:09.770 --> 01:06:11.468
actually do work well.

01:06:11.468 --> 01:06:13.260
But I would say that
as a practical matter,

01:06:13.260 --> 01:06:15.830
it's not that important.

01:06:15.830 --> 01:06:18.230
But we can-- we'll go--

01:06:18.230 --> 01:06:19.340
keep going.

01:06:19.340 --> 01:06:23.390
So when you, in the sense
of Nesterov and Nemirovski

01:06:23.390 --> 01:06:26.300
do their analysis, they end up
with something pretty crazy.

01:06:29.690 --> 01:06:32.700
You actually end up again--
it has exactly the same form.

01:06:32.700 --> 01:06:36.087
It says now you're using
the Newton decrement.

01:06:36.087 --> 01:06:38.670
In the classical analysis, we're
using a norm of the gradient.

01:06:38.670 --> 01:06:42.270
Norm of the gradient is
not affine independent.

01:06:42.270 --> 01:06:46.140
If I change coordinates,
then the norm of the gradient

01:06:46.140 --> 01:06:48.330
changes here.

01:06:48.330 --> 01:06:50.760
But remember, the Newton
decrement does not.

01:06:50.760 --> 01:06:52.920
That's an affine
invariant quantity.

01:06:52.920 --> 01:06:55.890
And so it says if
the Newton decrement

01:06:55.890 --> 01:07:00.390
is bigger than some number, then
you get a guaranteed decrease.

01:07:00.390 --> 01:07:03.220
Once on the other side, you
get something like that.

01:07:03.220 --> 01:07:04.620
So you get this very--

01:07:04.620 --> 01:07:07.530
this is a complexity bound
that looks like that.

01:07:07.530 --> 01:07:11.940
And yeah, these things depend
on the backtracking parameters.

01:07:11.940 --> 01:07:16.830
But a sloppy analysis tells
you that it ends up saying that

01:07:16.830 --> 01:07:20.370
the maximum number of iterations
is less than 375 times f of x0

01:07:20.370 --> 01:07:23.445
minus p star-- yes, that
issue is still there--

01:07:26.800 --> 01:07:28.164
plus 6.

01:07:28.164 --> 01:07:34.980
6 is the way I say log
log 1 over epsilon.

01:07:34.980 --> 01:07:36.437
In fact, I used
to give talks when

01:07:36.437 --> 01:07:38.520
I knew there were going
to be a bunch of theorists

01:07:38.520 --> 01:07:39.390
in the crowd.

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:44.580
I replaced log log with
4 and would do this just

01:07:44.580 --> 01:07:46.410
to taunt them.

01:07:46.410 --> 01:07:47.100
And I'd wait.

01:07:47.100 --> 01:07:48.870
And sure enough, I'd
see somebody itching,

01:07:48.870 --> 01:07:49.787
and they're twitching.

01:07:49.787 --> 01:07:53.160
And then they're like, wait,
this is an iterative algorithm.

01:07:53.160 --> 01:07:54.540
I was like, oh, I'm sorry.

01:07:54.540 --> 01:07:57.810
By 4, I meant log
log 1 over epsilon.

01:07:57.810 --> 01:07:59.730
Sorry, that's a macro for me.

01:07:59.730 --> 01:08:01.800
Anyway, it works pretty--

01:08:01.800 --> 01:08:02.760
I recommend this.

01:08:02.760 --> 01:08:05.310
If you know people like
that, you could try this too.

01:08:05.310 --> 01:08:07.590
So OK.

01:08:07.590 --> 01:08:10.060
All right, there it is.

01:08:10.060 --> 01:08:10.690
There you go.

01:08:10.690 --> 01:08:12.482
So you get actually
something that's weird.

01:08:12.482 --> 01:08:15.276
What's weird is that
these are actually not--

01:08:15.276 --> 01:08:16.859
I'll show you just
some examples here,

01:08:16.859 --> 01:08:20.606
but here's your minimizing a--

01:08:20.606 --> 01:08:24.285
you are minimizing a--

01:08:24.285 --> 01:08:27.779
it's a log barrier for a
set of linear inequalities

01:08:27.779 --> 01:08:28.680
or something.

01:08:28.680 --> 01:08:31.920
And then what we
did was we just--

01:08:31.920 --> 01:08:33.910
so to explain what we
did is we generated

01:08:33.910 --> 01:08:36.760
a sample, solved it
using Newton's method,

01:08:36.760 --> 01:08:39.279
noted the number of
iterations, and we also

01:08:39.279 --> 01:08:41.740
noted at that point p star.

01:08:41.740 --> 01:08:46.359
Then we could note this,
which is the difference.

01:08:46.359 --> 01:08:47.830
And then you just
scatter plot it.

01:08:47.830 --> 01:08:49.163
And you get something like this.

01:08:49.163 --> 01:08:51.430
Now, by the way,
the bound is a line

01:08:51.430 --> 01:08:54.040
that would be
almost vertical here

01:08:54.040 --> 01:08:57.010
because it's got this
coefficient 375 in there.

01:08:57.010 --> 01:09:00.040
But still, what's
interesting is-- actually,

01:09:00.040 --> 01:09:01.460
it's really interesting.

01:09:01.460 --> 01:09:06.729
It looks like if you're
deposed in a math courtroom

01:09:06.729 --> 01:09:11.500
or something like
that with scary people

01:09:11.500 --> 01:09:13.149
all around you
listening to everything

01:09:13.149 --> 01:09:15.316
you say, hoping you're going
to say something wrong,

01:09:15.316 --> 01:09:17.120
you use the number 375.

01:09:17.120 --> 01:09:18.189
It's fine.

01:09:18.189 --> 01:09:19.930
And then they would
say, is that true?

01:09:19.930 --> 01:09:23.109
And you go, oh,
that's actually true.

01:09:23.109 --> 01:09:26.529
But what's very
interesting here is

01:09:26.529 --> 01:09:30.520
that if you replace the
375 with 1/2, for example,

01:09:30.520 --> 01:09:33.399
it actually gets a pretty
good estimate of actually how

01:09:33.399 --> 01:09:35.859
many steps it's going to take.

01:09:35.859 --> 01:09:40.149
So this make sense?

01:09:40.149 --> 01:09:41.779
And it's actually pretty cool.

01:09:41.779 --> 01:09:45.819
So you can actually give
a talk to two audiences,

01:09:45.819 --> 01:09:47.920
and they can even
be mixed together.

01:09:47.920 --> 01:09:50.620
Because you just put
a constant in front.

01:09:50.620 --> 01:09:52.060
You just say it's this.

01:09:52.060 --> 01:09:54.793
And then if someone says,
what is a true statement,

01:09:54.793 --> 01:09:56.710
you'd say, well, to make
this a true statement

01:09:56.710 --> 01:09:58.360
I'd have to say 375.

01:09:58.360 --> 01:10:02.140
But if you wanted to say, here's
something that is probably true

01:10:02.140 --> 01:10:04.900
and a good approximation,
you just change c to 0.5,

01:10:04.900 --> 01:10:06.580
and you're done.

01:10:06.580 --> 01:10:09.250
So OK.

01:10:09.250 --> 01:10:10.930
It's cool.

01:10:10.930 --> 01:10:13.060
So this is how that works.

01:10:13.060 --> 01:10:20.890
Oh, by the way, can
someone explain this one?

01:10:20.890 --> 01:10:24.748
So here, this difference was--

01:10:24.748 --> 01:10:25.290
I don't know.

01:10:25.290 --> 01:10:26.790
Let's say it was 12.

01:10:26.790 --> 01:10:32.370
But it converged in
eight iterations.

01:10:32.370 --> 01:10:35.040
What word would you say if
someone said, explain that?

01:10:38.865 --> 01:10:39.810
What happened there?

01:10:44.350 --> 01:10:47.890
How about just dumb luck?

01:10:47.890 --> 01:10:49.360
There you are in some--

01:10:49.360 --> 01:10:51.730
I don't even know what the
dimension of these things is.

01:10:51.730 --> 01:10:52.510
OK.

01:10:52.510 --> 01:10:53.260
I have no idea.

01:10:53.260 --> 01:10:55.610
It's either 50 variables
or 500 variables.

01:10:55.610 --> 01:10:56.360
It doesn't matter.

01:10:56.360 --> 01:10:58.777
But the point was you asked
for directions, went that way.

01:10:58.777 --> 01:11:02.410
You asked again, seventh time
you asked for directions.

01:11:02.410 --> 01:11:04.870
And then by dumb
luck it just happened

01:11:04.870 --> 01:11:07.270
to point exactly
towards the minimum.

01:11:07.270 --> 01:11:09.796
so So that's what those are.

01:11:09.796 --> 01:11:12.310
Those are just dumb luck.

01:11:12.310 --> 01:11:13.510
Yeah?

01:11:13.510 --> 01:11:17.770
On the previous slide,
what was lambda of x?

01:11:17.770 --> 01:11:18.370
What's lambda?

01:11:18.370 --> 01:11:21.400
Lambda is this Newton decrement.

01:11:21.400 --> 01:11:24.610
So that's actually-- it's this.

01:11:24.610 --> 01:11:28.420
It's the gradient here.

01:11:32.680 --> 01:11:35.620
Actually, it's something
like this divided by 2.

01:11:35.620 --> 01:11:38.215
Actually, lambda is the
square root of this.

01:11:38.215 --> 01:11:39.730
This is very close.

01:11:39.730 --> 01:11:42.200
Lambda-- here, let
me put it-- yeah, OK.

01:11:42.200 --> 01:11:42.700
Here.

01:11:42.700 --> 01:11:45.230
Lambda is something like that.

01:11:45.230 --> 01:11:49.818
And there's a factor
of 2 in there, roughly.

01:11:49.818 --> 01:11:51.860
I can't remember where
the square root of 2 goes,

01:11:51.860 --> 01:11:53.615
but that's what it is.

01:11:53.615 --> 01:11:54.990
Actually, it's
super interesting.

01:11:54.990 --> 01:12:00.380
It is the size of the gradient
in the current Hessian

01:12:00.380 --> 01:12:04.320
norm, which is pretty--

01:12:04.320 --> 01:12:06.500
and that's how you
can guess that it's

01:12:06.500 --> 01:12:08.900
going to be affine invariant.

01:12:08.900 --> 01:12:12.210
So OK.

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:14.133
So we looked at this a bit.

01:12:14.133 --> 01:12:16.300
And now we'll say a little
bit about implementation.

01:12:16.300 --> 01:12:18.020
This is actually
far more important.

01:12:18.020 --> 01:12:19.020
And this is interesting.

01:12:19.020 --> 01:12:20.395
Because now we're
going to tie it

01:12:20.395 --> 01:12:25.800
to last week, which is
numerical linear algebra.

01:12:25.800 --> 01:12:29.610
So basically, what it says
is let's review where we are.

01:12:29.610 --> 01:12:31.110
At a high level you
would say this--

01:12:31.110 --> 01:12:33.433
how do you minimize a
smooth convex function?

01:12:33.433 --> 01:12:34.850
And the answer is
Newton's method.

01:12:34.850 --> 01:12:36.225
You say, well,
what does that do?

01:12:36.225 --> 01:12:39.030
You say that converts
the solution.

01:12:39.030 --> 01:12:41.850
How do you solve a
smooth convex function

01:12:41.850 --> 01:12:44.850
is we don't know how
to do that, but we

01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:49.170
do know how to minimize a
convex quadratic function.

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:50.100
That's easy.

01:12:50.100 --> 01:12:51.690
That's actually linear algebra.

01:12:51.690 --> 01:12:54.640
That's solving a set
of linear equations.

01:12:54.640 --> 01:12:56.520
So you could say that
Newton's method reduces

01:12:56.520 --> 01:13:01.050
the solution of the
minimization of a smooth convex

01:13:01.050 --> 01:13:04.170
function to
minimizing a sequence

01:13:04.170 --> 01:13:05.550
of quadratic functions.

01:13:05.550 --> 01:13:09.150
And someone says, OK, how do you
minimize a quadratic function?

01:13:09.150 --> 01:13:11.190
You go, oh, that's
linear algebra.

01:13:11.190 --> 01:13:12.430
That's called linear algebra.

01:13:12.430 --> 01:13:13.670
Everybody following this?

01:13:13.670 --> 01:13:15.760
So when you roll it
up, it says that we

01:13:15.760 --> 01:13:20.590
can minimize a smooth convex
function by an iteration that's

01:13:20.590 --> 01:13:23.140
going to take some number of
tens or whatever of iterations.

01:13:23.140 --> 01:13:27.070
Each iteration is
linear algebra.

01:13:27.070 --> 01:13:29.360
And in fact, the linear
algebra is very simple.

01:13:29.360 --> 01:13:32.630
You're just solving
this Newton system,

01:13:32.630 --> 01:13:34.705
H times delta x equals minus g.

01:13:37.840 --> 01:13:41.770
One way to do it is just
by Cholesky factorization.

01:13:41.770 --> 01:13:43.840
So you do a Cholesky
factorization.

01:13:43.840 --> 01:13:45.520
And that'll cost
you n cubed flops

01:13:45.520 --> 01:13:46.645
for an unstructured system.

01:13:50.110 --> 01:13:51.130
OK.

01:13:51.130 --> 01:13:52.640
But here's the interesting part.

01:13:52.640 --> 01:13:58.300
If the Hessian is
structured and you

01:13:58.300 --> 01:14:02.470
know how to exploit it,
or even better basically

01:14:02.470 --> 01:14:07.162
you know how to call the
right libraries to exploit

01:14:07.162 --> 01:14:08.620
it or something
like that, which is

01:14:08.620 --> 01:14:11.800
what-- that's what I mean when I
say you know how to exploit it.

01:14:11.800 --> 01:14:16.720
If that's the case, then this
is going to work really well.

01:14:16.720 --> 01:14:20.590
Let me just give a couple of
examples about how that works.

01:14:24.880 --> 01:14:25.630
We could do--

01:14:25.630 --> 01:14:30.750
I mean, it doesn't matter,
but we could do some kind of--

01:14:30.750 --> 01:14:33.490
we'll just do some kind of
signal processing problem

01:14:33.490 --> 01:14:37.180
or optimal control problem where
you're estimating something,

01:14:37.180 --> 01:14:39.030
but it's a--

01:14:39.030 --> 01:14:44.470
in that case, the variables
only interact locally

01:14:44.470 --> 01:14:46.000
in these types of--

01:14:46.000 --> 01:14:48.400
like in a control problem
or something like that.

01:14:48.400 --> 01:14:51.940
What'll happen
then is the Hessian

01:14:51.940 --> 01:14:58.860
is then going to be banded
because variable x doesn't

01:14:58.860 --> 01:15:02.100
interact with
variable xi and xj.

01:15:02.100 --> 01:15:07.350
They only interact if i
minus j is small enough, OK?

01:15:07.350 --> 01:15:10.620
That means, if you go back
to last week, that says,

01:15:10.620 --> 01:15:13.320
oh, we can solve those
systems in linear time.

01:15:13.320 --> 01:15:16.080
I would not be afraid to solve--

01:15:16.080 --> 01:15:18.900
I can compute the Newton
direction if H was banded,

01:15:18.900 --> 01:15:20.610
let's say, with a
bandwidth of 20.

01:15:20.610 --> 01:15:24.658
If it's in a million dimensions,
I could solve that problem.

01:15:24.658 --> 01:15:26.700
This, I guarantee you,
people in machine learning

01:15:26.700 --> 01:15:30.060
have absolutely not
the slightest idea of.

01:15:30.060 --> 01:15:33.900
There might be one or two, but
I doubt any more than that.

01:15:33.900 --> 01:15:37.650
OK, maybe a few more,
but not that many.

01:15:37.650 --> 01:15:40.240
And that's actually
part of the reason--

01:15:40.240 --> 01:15:42.652
it is very interesting
when people say, oh, you

01:15:42.652 --> 01:15:43.860
couldn't use Newton's method.

01:15:43.860 --> 01:15:44.360
Why not?

01:15:44.360 --> 01:15:47.040
You go, well, first of all,
you have to form this Hessian.

01:15:47.040 --> 01:15:49.020
And second, then you
have to solve it.

01:15:49.020 --> 01:15:50.650
And everybody knows
that's n cubed.

01:15:50.650 --> 01:15:53.100
But the point is if it's
structured, it's not.

01:15:53.100 --> 01:15:54.212
So OK.

01:15:54.212 --> 01:15:56.420
And then you'd say, how
often does structure come up?

01:15:56.420 --> 01:15:58.840
And the answer is a
lot more frequently

01:15:58.840 --> 01:16:00.790
than you would imagine.

01:16:00.790 --> 01:16:03.130
I mean, that also makes sense.

01:16:03.130 --> 01:16:03.670
OK.

01:16:03.670 --> 01:16:08.920
So this is Newton method,
how you would actually

01:16:08.920 --> 01:16:09.895
implement it.

01:16:09.895 --> 01:16:11.770
Oh, by the way, I
mentioned this other method

01:16:11.770 --> 01:16:16.990
where you update
basically the quadratic.

01:16:16.990 --> 01:16:19.990
What you do is you
calculate the Hessian once.

01:16:19.990 --> 01:16:23.210
And you use that as your--
you use that for the next,

01:16:23.210 --> 01:16:25.642
let's say, 10 or 20 steps.

01:16:25.642 --> 01:16:26.850
You don't update the Hessian.

01:16:26.850 --> 01:16:28.260
You update it every 20 steps.

01:16:28.260 --> 01:16:29.760
It's got a name for
it, and I forgot

01:16:29.760 --> 01:16:32.970
what that method is called.

01:16:32.970 --> 01:16:34.800
How do you do that here?

01:16:34.800 --> 01:16:38.310
And what's the cost of that?

01:16:38.310 --> 01:16:40.650
This is completely dense,
no structure in the Hessian.

01:16:40.650 --> 01:16:42.570
What do you do?

01:16:42.570 --> 01:16:45.720
Every 20 steps, you evaluate
the Hessian, factor it.

01:16:45.720 --> 01:16:47.640
That costs you n cubed.

01:16:47.640 --> 01:16:50.460
Then how much do actually
computing the Newton steps

01:16:50.460 --> 01:16:53.070
when you have different
gradients here?

01:16:56.010 --> 01:16:56.880
What's the number?

01:16:56.880 --> 01:16:57.960
N squared.

01:16:57.960 --> 01:16:59.220
It's n squared, exactly.

01:16:59.220 --> 01:17:02.745
So if n is big,
that's a big savings.

01:17:02.745 --> 01:17:04.620
By the way, that method
works perfectly well.

01:17:04.620 --> 01:17:05.610
People don't
implement it because I

01:17:05.610 --> 01:17:08.130
think they're just lazy, so
they just factor every time.

01:17:08.130 --> 01:17:11.280
But it's an old method
from the '70s or '80s,

01:17:11.280 --> 01:17:13.950
and it's perfectly good.

01:17:13.950 --> 01:17:16.300
Probably people should use it.

01:17:16.300 --> 01:17:16.800
OK.

01:17:16.800 --> 01:17:18.540
So here's a quick example.

01:17:18.540 --> 01:17:23.343
But I think what we're going to
do is we're going to quit here.

01:17:23.343 --> 01:17:24.510
And we'll quit here for now.

01:17:24.510 --> 01:17:28.540
And then I think there's a--

01:17:28.540 --> 01:17:29.790
let me just mention one thing.

01:17:29.790 --> 01:17:31.415
You're doing something
on the homework.

01:17:31.415 --> 01:17:33.060
And you're actually--
we're only going

01:17:33.060 --> 01:17:35.160
to cover the material
for that next Tuesday.

01:17:35.160 --> 01:17:37.650
So you'll have to read
